course: language
lesson: 43
title: Pan, panic and Ulysses: the old myths and old words inside «The Wind in the Willows»
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: In the chapter «The Piper at the Gates of Dawn», the Rat and the Mole find a being with curved horns, shaggy legs and hooves, playing pipes, with little Portly asleep at his feet. The book never names him, but he is Pan, a god from the myths of ancient Greece.
explain: The old Greeks said Pan lived in wild places and that the sudden wild fear people felt alone in the hills came from him; that is where our word «panic» comes from. Grahame changed the myth and made Pan a kind «Friend and Helper», which writers are allowed to do.
explain: The last chapter, «The Return of Ulysses», borrows another myth: the hero Ulysses comes home after years away and finds strangers feasting in his hall, just as Toad finds the weasels at their banquet in Toad Hall.
explain: Many everyday English words were born in these old stories: panic, odyssey, mentor, echo, cereal and even the month of January.
explain: So a myth is not only a story: it is a store-room of words that we still use every day.
fact: In Greek myth Pan was the god of the wild, of shepherds and their flocks; he was shown with a man's body, goat's horns and goat's legs and hooves.
fact: In one myth a nymph called Syrinx ran from Pan and was turned into reeds by a river; Pan cut reeds of different lengths, tied them together and made the first pan-pipes.
fact: In pan-pipes the shorter pipes play higher notes and the longer pipes play lower notes, because a shorter column of air vibrates faster.
fact: The Greeks said the sudden, wild fear people felt in lonely places was sent by Pan, so it was called «panic»; the book says carefully that the Mole felt awe, not panic.
fact: The Romans had a similar god of the woods called Faunus, and a goddess of flowers called Flora; from them come «fauna», meaning the animals of a place, and «flora», meaning its plants.
fact: Peter Pan, the boy who never grows up, was named after the god Pan by J. M. Barrie, and he too plays pipes in the woods.
fact: Ulysses is the Roman name for the Greek hero Odysseus; Homer's poem the «Odyssey» tells how he took ten years to sail home after the Trojan War.
fact: When Odysseus got home, many men were feasting in his hall, eating his food and wanting to marry his wife Penelope; he came in dressed as a beggar and, with his son and loyal helpers, drove them out, which is why Grahame named Toad's chapter after him.
fact: An «odyssey» now means any long, adventurous journey, and a «mentor», a wise guide, comes from Mentor, the old friend of Odysseus whose shape the goddess Athena took to guide his son.
fact: «Echo» comes from a nymph called Echo who could only repeat the last words others said; «cereal» comes from Ceres, the Roman goddess of grain; January is named after Janus, the Roman god of doorways with two faces.
fact: «Dulce Domum», the title of the Mole's chapter, is Latin for «sweet home»; the Latin «domus», a house, also gave us «domestic», meaning to do with the home.
fact: In 1967 the band Pink Floyd named their first album «The Piper at the Gates of Dawn» after this chapter of the book.
exercise: Who is the Piper in the book? | Pan, a god from Greek myth | Peter Pan, the boy who never grows up | A shepherd from the village | The Otter in disguise
exercise: What did Pan look like in Greek myth? | A man with goat's horns, legs and hooves | A huge bird with golden wings and a lion's tail | A small boy dressed in green leaves | A horse with the head of a man
exercise: What was Pan the god of? | The wild, shepherds and their flocks | The sea, the storms and the earthquakes | The sky, the thunder and the lightning | Cooking, baking and bread
exercise: What was Syrinx turned into? | Reeds by a river | A tall willow tree | A stone in the hills | A singing bird
exercise: In pan-pipes, which pipes play the highest notes? | The shortest ones | The longest ones | The widest ones at the end | The ones with the biggest holes
exercise: Where does the word «panic» come from? | The god Pan, said to send sudden wild fear | A frying pan dropped in the kitchen with a crash | A Roman city that burnt down in one night | An old word for running away
exercise: What did the Mole feel on the island, according to the book? | Awe, not panic | Panic, not awe | Only hunger | Nothing at all
exercise: What does «fauna» mean? | The animals of a place | The wild plants of a place | The flowing rivers of a place | The usual weather of a place
exercise: Who named Peter Pan after the god? | J. M. Barrie | Kenneth Grahame | Lewis Carroll | Homer
exercise: What is the Greek name of Ulysses? | Odysseus | Hercules | Achilles | Zeus
exercise: How long did Odysseus take to get home after the Trojan War? | Ten years | Just ten days | One hundred years | A single night
exercise: What did Odysseus find in his hall when he got home? | Men feasting, eating his food and wanting to marry his wife | His wife and his son waiting quietly for him all alone | Weasels and stoats singing songs about him | A dragon guarding his treasure
exercise: What is an odyssey today? | A long, adventurous journey | A short walk to the shops | A kind of Greek boat | A very loud song
exercise: What is a mentor? | A wise guide | A Greek soldier | A sea monster | A kind of harp
exercise: Which word comes from Ceres, goddess of grain? | Cereal | Ceremony | Certain | Cellophane
exercise: What does «Dulce Domum» mean? | Sweet home | Hard, stony road | Deep, dark river | Wild, dark wood
exercise: What did Pink Floyd name after a chapter of the book? | Their first album | Their big tour bus | Their pet dog | Their favourite guitar
test: «the piper's just some random goat guy right» What is the best answer? | He is Pan, the Greek god of the wild, though the book never says his name | He is a farmer who has dressed up as a goat for a party in the village | He is the Badger wearing a costume he made for the summer | He is a real goat that has learnt to play music from the shepherds
test: A friend says the word «panic» comes from people dropping frying pans. What is right? | It comes from the god Pan, who was said to send sudden fear | Yes, it comes from frying pans clattering on stone kitchen floors | It comes from a city called Panic in Italy | It comes from the sound a scared duck makes
test: In the myth Pan frightens people; in the book he is a kind «Friend and Helper». What does that show? | A writer can borrow a myth and change it | The book got the myth wrong by mistake | Pan became kinder as he got older | The Greeks never told any stories about Pan at all
test: You blow across a short pipe and a long pipe. Which sounds higher? | The short pipe | The long pipe | Both sound the same | Neither makes a sound
test: «why is the last chapter called the return of ulysses, there's no ulysses in it» What is the best answer? | Like Ulysses, Toad comes home and drives out the strangers feasting in his hall | Ulysses is the real name of the Badger, which he keeps secret from his friends | Ulysses was the name of Toad's father, who built Toad Hall | It is a mistake by the printer
test: Mole goes to the stoats dressed as a washerwoman before the battle. Which part of the Odyssey is like that? | Odysseus came home dressed as a beggar | Odysseus fought a dragon in the hall | Odysseus sold his horse to a gipsy for gold | Odysseus stole a chariot from the king
test: «my cousin's gap year was a proper odyssey» What does she mean? | It was a long, adventurous journey | It was a short, boring week at home | She read a Greek poem all year | She sailed to Troy in a wooden horse
test: A nature programme talks about «the flora and fauna of the river bank». What is it about? | The plants and animals of the river bank | The boats and bridges of the river bank | The weather and seasons of the river | Two famous Roman sisters who lived there
test: Which word does NOT come from a myth? | Window | Panic | Echo | Cereal
test: «Dulce Domum» is about the Mole longing for his home. Which English word comes from the same Latin «domus»? | Domestic | Dormitory | Dandelion | Doodle
test: ★★★ A set of pan-pipes has 8 pipes. The longest is 24 cm and each next pipe is 2 cm shorter. How long is the shortest pipe? | 10 cm | 8 cm | 16 cm | 12 cm
exam: Identify the figure of Greek myth who is represented, though not named, in the chapter «The Piper at the Gates of Dawn». | Pan, deity of wild places, of shepherds and of their flocks | Poseidon, ruler of the sea, of horses and of earthquakes | Hermes, messenger of the gods | Apollo, god of the sun
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of the English word «panic». | It derives from the god Pan, to whom sudden irrational terror was attributed | It derives from an implement of the kitchen, which made a great noise when dropped upon stone | It derives from the name of a Roman emperor | It derives from an Old English word for haste
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the final chapter of the novel bears the title «The Return of Ulysses». | The hero, returning home, expels the strangers feasting in his hall, as did Odysseus | The chapter recounts a voyage to Troy undertaken by the Water Rat and the Mole in the Rat's boat | The Badger is revealed to be a Greek king who has been living in disguise | Toad visits Greece upon a motor tour
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term «mentor»? | It denotes a wise guide and derives from a character in the Odyssey | It denotes a musical instrument made of reeds and played by the shepherds of Arcadia | It denotes a sea monster of Greek myth | It denotes a Roman coin
